Day 22: LangChain Components

Recap: LangChain
Open source framework for LLM powered applications.
Advantages: build pipelines, chain concept (one output feeds the next), very flexible.
Examples: chatbot, AI knowledge assistants.

LangChain components:
1. Models
2. Prompts
3. Chains
4. Memory
5. Indexes
6. Agents

1. Models: interact with AI models.
Before LLMs, chatbot context awareness and text generation were weak. LLMs solved it.
But LLMs are trained on huge data and are very hard to manage.
LLMs cannot easily run locally, and running on your own server gives a high bill.
So companies expose APIs and we just use them.
Next problem: implementation. Using OpenAI needs one code style, Claude another.
Standardization was hard, and even the output structure differs between providers.
LangChain fixed this issue with a common interface.

LangChain has two types of models:
1. Language models: give text, get text (LLMs).
2. Embedding models: give text, get vector output. Mainly used for semantic search.

2. Prompts: input provided to the LLM.
Prompts are very important. A small change to a prompt can change the output.
Prompts even have jobs now: prompt engineering.
LangChain supports different types of prompts:
Dynamic and reusable prompts, e.g. summarize (topic) in (emotion) tone.
Role based prompting.
Few shot prompting.

3. Chains: LangChain was even named after chains.
We can make pipelines from chains. Think of visual pipeline tools like n8n.
Input -> LLM -> translate -> LLM -> summary in 100 words -> output.
Advantage: output of the first step is the input of the second.
Complex pipelines are possible:
Parallel chains: input goes to LLM 1, LLM 2, LLM 3, then combine all outputs.
Conditional chains: like if else or switch conditions.

4. Indexes: connect the application to external knowledge such as PDF, website, or database.
Main parts of indexes:
1. Document loader: bring data into the system.
2. Text splitter: split 100 pages into pages, chapters, or paragraphs.
3. Vector store: store embedded vectors.
4. Retriever: convert user query to a vector, run semantic search between query and page vectors, give the best pages to the LLM to generate text.
Example: ChatGPT cannot know your company details, like the leave policy.
Connect it to your company PDF and now it can answer those questions.

5. Memory: LLM API calls are stateless.
First query: I am Bishal. Second query: who am I? The LLM will not remember.
Types of memory:
Conversation buffer memory: store the transcript of recent conversation. Problem: very long conversations get hard.
Conversation buffer window memory: keep only the last N interactions.
Summarizer based memory: keep a summary of the conversation.
Custom memory: keep advanced data about the user.

6. Agents: an LLM can generate text but it cannot do work.
Agents can do work, for example with the help of MCP.
Example: an LLM can suggest a trip to Pokhara for a holiday.
An agent can also book the ticket to Pokhara.
AI agents have two powers:
1. Reasoning capabilities.
2. Tool access.
